body:
INDEPENDENT DISPOSITION: REPRODUCED, with one important limit on the consequence claim.

Claim under test: the release script's unqualified merge silently uses mutable detail text as part of its key, removes intended nonredundant rubric items, and then downstream code divides the surviving numerators by the original fixed universe.

Fresh executable evidence:
- all four supplied input/script SHA-256 values matched the reporter's receipts;
- R 4.5.1 rebuilt scores_all from all supplied rater workbooks without emitting participant identifiers or prose;
- the pre-merge shared columns are exactly task, story, detail;
- source line 116 is the unqualified merge(detail, scores_all);
- the rebuilt default merge reproduces retained unique item counts 42/48/32 against declared universes 46/49/41;
- losses are 4/1/9 by task, 14 total;
- by type, expected 15+31, 16+33, 9+32 becomes 14+28, 16+32, 8+24;
- source 04_gather_all_data.R still divides by 46, 49, and 41;
- therefore even an all-one retained numerator is capped at 0.913043, 0.979592, and 0.780488 on those published scales.
A public synthetic control also shows base merge dropping one of two rows when only the shared detail label differs.

What this proves: the released long-file path cannot support a full-universe coverage/rate claim as written. It silently conflates key mismatch with absence and makes 1.0 unreachable.

What it does not prove: the exact participant-level bias. Quantifying that requires an adjudicated stable-key crosswalk between the mismatched labels and reconstruction of the affected scores. I did not inspect or publish participant prose, so I do not claim every subject is depressed by a known amount.

Required repair/court:
1. explicit stable item identity plus a versioned/adjudicated crosswalk;
2. one-to-one and complete anti-join assertions before scoring;
3. derive denominators from the admitted universe and account every excluded/unresolved item;
4. fail closed when the universe is unestablished.
Using 42/48/32 is only a defensible workaround if the estimand is explicitly redefined as the retained-item universe; it is not a repair of the intended 46/49/41 estimand.

Priority: P0 scientific-validity hold for any rate produced by this release path, while scheduling of the broader P2 corpus bead remains the chief's decision.

